## The Problem: Object Reference Equality

This is a very common React issue. When you pass an object as a prop, React compares props by **reference**, not by value. A new object literal creates a new reference every render, even if the contents are identical.

```javascript
// ❌ This creates a NEW object reference on every render
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <Child config={{ theme: "dark", size: "lg" }} /> // New object every render!
  );
}
```

---

## Solutions

### 1. Move the object outside the component (if static)
```javascript
// ✅ Created once, reference never changes
const CONFIG = { theme: "dark", size: "lg" };

function Parent() {
  return <Child config={CONFIG} />;
}
```

### 2. useMemo (if the object depends on state/props)
```javascript
function Parent({ theme }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ✅ Only recreates when `theme` changes
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme,
    size: "lg"
  }), [theme]);

  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

### 3. Wrap the child in React.memo
```javascript
// ✅ Skips re-render if props are shallowly equal
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});
```

### 4. Custom comparison with React.memo
```javascript
// ✅ Deep comparison (use carefully - has its own cost)
const Child = React.memo(
  function Child({ config }) {
    return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
  },
  (prevProps, nextProps) => {
    return prevProps.config.theme === nextProps.config.theme &&
           prevProps.config.size === nextProps.config.size;
  }
);
```

### 5. Pass primitive values instead of objects
```javascript
// ✅ Primitives compare by value, not reference
function Parent({ theme, size }) {
  return <Child theme={theme} size={size} />;
}
```

---

## Key Decision Guide

| Situation | Solution |
|-----------|----------|
| Object never changes | Move outside component |
| Object depends on props/state | `useMemo` |
| Child is expensive to render | `React.memo` |
| Need deep comparison | `React.memo` with custom comparator |
| Can split the object | Pass primitives instead |

---

## Important Caveats

- **`React.memo` alone isn't enough** if the parent keeps creating new object references — the shallow comparison will still fail
- **Don't over-optimize** — React re-renders are fast; only optimize when you measure an actual performance problem
- **`useMemo` has overhead too** — it's not free, so use it when the render cost is genuinely high

The most robust fix is usually combining `useMemo` in the parent **with** `React.memo` on the child.
